<!-- S3: EXPLANATION TAX + PERTURBATION -->
<section class="slide s3" id="s3"><div class="inr wide">
<div class="pulse">03 · Explanation Tax & Recovery</div>
<h2>Narration costs 24–120%.<br>Perturbation reveals who recovers silently.</h2>
<div class="cards">
<div class="card gn" onclick="this.classList.toggle('expanded')"><div class="cname">DeepSeek v4 Pro</div><div class="cval gn">24.4%</div><div class="csub">Natural $0.016 → Forced $0.013</div><div class="ctag gn">Silent Inference ✅</div><div class="detail"><strong>Recovery Multiplier:</strong> 0.9× (perturbation <em>reduces</em> cost)<br><strong>Test Pass Rate:</strong> 25/41 (61%) — FastAPI architectural choice, 11/11 (100%) with Flask-only<br><strong>Reasoning:</strong> 386 chars · GRPO compresses into latent space<br><strong>88 experiment sessions · $1.32 total cost</strong></div></div>
<div class="card rd" onclick="this.classList.toggle('expanded')"><div class="cname">Claude Fable 5</div><div class="cval rd">52.5%</div><div class="csub">Natural $0.249 → Forced $0.163</div><div class="ctag rd">Explanation Tax ⚠️</div><div class="detail"><strong>Recovery Multiplier:</strong> 1.1× (perturbation <em>increases</em> cost)<br><strong>Test Pass Rate:</strong> 54/54 (100%)<br><strong>Reasoning:</strong> 1,639 chars · Imitation+RLHF = verbose<br><strong>30 experiment sessions · $27.66 total cost</strong></div></div>
<div class="card am" onclick="this.classList.toggle('expanded')"><div class="cname">GPT-5.6</div><div class="cval am">50.1%</div><div class="csub">Natural $0.464 → Forced $0.309</div><div class="ctag am">Moderate Overhead</div><div class="detail"><strong>Test Pass Rate:</strong> 16/16 (100%)<br><strong>Reasoning:</strong> 1,167 chars<br><strong>15 experiment sessions · $6.71 total cost<br>Cross-domain validated: Task Manager, Data Table, Collaborative Editor</strong></div></div>
<div class="card rd" onclick="this.classList.toggle('expanded')"><div class="cname">GPT-5-mini</div><div class="cval rd">119.6%</div><div class="csub">Natural $0.060 → Forced $0.027</div><div class="ctag rd">Over-Explainer</div><div class="detail"><strong>Test Pass Rate:</strong> 4/4 (100%)<br><strong>Forced-silent cuts cost by 55%</strong> · Smaller models compensate with verbosity<br><strong>12 experiment sessions · $0.36 total cost</strong></div></div>
</div>
<div class="stats" style="margin-top:1rem"><div class="stat"><div class="v ac">10</div><div class="l">Perturbation Operators</div><div class="s">5 manifold (alien vocab, framing shifts) + 5 semantic (missing constraints, false premises, phantom success)</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="v gn">205</div><div class="l">Sessions Analyzed</div><div class="s">Constraint markers spike 10× under perturbation — correctly classifies perturbed vs baseline</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="v rd">0.9×</div><div class="l">DS Recovery ×</div><div class="s">Perturbation reduces cost — silent recovery wins</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="v am">1.1×</div><div class="l">Claude Recovery ×</div><div class="s">Perturbation increases cost — pays to explain recovery</div></div></div>
<p class="note">AST analysis confirms: perturbed code output is <strong style="color:var(--gn)">112.6% of baseline AST nodes</strong> — structurally identical. The tax is purely from narration, not from rework.</p>
<div class="wtm"><strong>What This Means:</strong> <span class="role cto">CTO</span> Perturbation reveals model resilience strategy — DeepSeek recovers in latent space, Claude recovers in prose. <span class="role cfo">CFO</span> DeepSeek's 0.9× multiplier means perturbation <em>saves</em> money; Claude's 1.1× means it costs extra. <span class="role coo">COO</span> Treat forced-silent as the default production mode; request explanations only for audit checkpoints.</div>
<a href="#s4" class="cta-btn ghost">The complete optimization strategy →</a>
</div></section>

<!-- S4: STRATEGY STACK -->
<section class="slide s4" id="s4"><div class="inr">
<div class="pulse">04 · The Strategy Stack</div>
<h2>5 levels of AI cost optimization.<br>One unified equation.</h2>
<p class="lead">Every cost lever maps to a single parameter in the FinOps equation. From model selection to energy hedging — one framework governs all five levels.</p>
<div class="strat-stack">
<div class="sr"><div class="num gn">1</div><div class="txt"><strong>Model Selection</strong> — Route each task to the cheapest model that passes tests for that task type.<span class="lev">Lever: C₀ · Measured by: Cost per successful outcome · Two-way door — experiment fast</span></div></div>
<div class="sr"><div class="num ac">2</div><div class="txt"><strong>Reasoning Governance</strong> — Apply Silent Inference for routine tasks. Use explicit reasoning only for complex architectural decisions.<span class="lev">Lever: ϵ (Explanation Tax 24–120%) · Two-way door — toggle per query</span></div></div>
<div class="sr"><div class="num am">3</div><div class="txt"><strong>Architectural Efficiency</strong> — Favor MoE over dense models. Active params (not total) determine true per-token cost.<span class="lev">Lever: A (13.5× Claude/DS) · One-way door — multi-month migration</span></div></div>
<div class="sr"><div class="num rd">4</div><div class="txt"><strong>Codebase Growth Control</strong> — Context inflation compounds quadratically via the N² term. Refactor to control β. Pin dependencies to prevent bad architectural choices.<span class="lev">Lever: β, v · 10% β error → 50% cost overrun after 100 sessions</span></div></div>
<div class="sr"><div class="num pu">5</div><div class="txt"><strong>Energy Price Hedging</strong> — Lock in today's efficient-model costs before EPM rises 1.8× by 2050.<span class="lev">Lever: EPM(t) · Sources: IEA, Goldman Sachs, EIA, Rystad Energy</span></div></div>
</div>
<a href="#s5" class="cta-btn ghost">One-Way vs Two-Way Doors: the decision framework →</a>
</div></section>

<!-- S5: DECISION FRAMEWORK -->
<section class="slide s5" id="s5"><div class="inr">
<div class="pulse">05 · Decision Framework</div>
<h2>One-Way vs Two-Way Doors.<br>When to act fast. When to model rigorously.</h2>
<div class="doors">
<div class="door tw"><h3>Two-Way Doors · Act Fast</h3><p>Decisions that change <strong style="color:var(--gn)">C₀ alone</strong> — easily reversible. The N² term doesn't lock you in.</p><ul><li>Switching models for a single task</li><li>Turning Silent Inference on/off</li><li>Adjusting max_tokens or caching</li><li>A/B testing a new model</li></ul><p style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--gn)">Rule: Experiment. Measure C₀. Switch fast.</p></div>
<div class="door ow"><h3>One-Way Doors · Model Rigorously</h3><p>Decisions that change <strong style="color:var(--rd)">β, v, or EPM</strong> — compound through the N² term.</p><ul><li>Choosing MoE vs dense as primary architecture</li><li>3-year reserved instance commitments</li><li>Building custom agentic frameworks</li><li>Selecting a primary cloud provider</li><li>Exclusive enterprise model contracts</li></ul><p style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--rd)">Rule: Use the cumulative cost equation. Project N over 12–36 months.</p></div>
</div>
<p class="note" style="max-width:600px;margin:1rem auto"><strong>The hidden trap:</strong> Model routing looks like a two-way door (switch per query) but at scale becomes a one-way door — your routing architecture locks you into a distribution of models that can change on provider schedules. Invest heavily in the routing architecture. Experiment freely with individual route assignments.</p>
<div class="wtm"><strong>What This Means:</strong> <span class="role cto">CTO</span> Invest heavily in your routing architecture (one-way door). Experiment freely with individual route assignments (two-way door). <span class="role cfo">CFO</span> A 10% error in β today becomes a 50% cost overrun after 100 sessions — one-way door decisions need proper financial modeling.</div>
<a href="#s6" class="cta-btn ghost">The evidence: 8 models, real test data →</a>
</div></section>

